        # This counts each triangle exactly once because we iterate through nodes and their neighbor pairs.
        # A triangle (a, b, c) will be counted when processing 'a' (checking pair b,c), 
        # when processing 'b' (checking pair a,c), and when processing 'c' (checking pair a,b).
        # Wait, this logic counts each triangle 3 times. We need to divide by 3 or change the approach.

        # Let's re-evaluate: The problem asks for distinct cycles. A cycle is defined by its set of vertices.
